## Introduction
In recent years, with the continuing development and progress of nanotechnology, heterogeneous nanomaterials, constructed by two nanoparticles of unique nature [l],have been created as an important class of_composite  nanostructures for energy storage [2], sensing [3],catalysis [4,5], and cancer therapy [6,7]. One prominent characteristicof  these heterogeneous nanomaterials is their exquisitely multifunctional properties due to cooperative functions, which are greater than the physical mixtures of all components [8]. Therefore, many types of heterogeneous nanomaterials have been designed and fabricated to achieve enhanced and synergistic performances in the past years [9-12].
Among various types of building components, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), synthesized by self-assembly of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands through coordination bonds, have emerged as powerful platforms for many applications, attributing to their tunable structural features and pore sizes, high surface areas, good biocompatibility, and versatile functionality [13-18]. Consequently, heterogeneous nanomaterials based on MOFs have attracted more and more research interests in recent years [19-23]. In fact, combining MOFs with other nanomaterials can not only surmount the inherent limitations of individual materials, but also endow MOFs with new functions [1,8,24]. Particularly, composites of MOF with optically active nanorods exhibit distinct optical properties, which makes them attractive for applications in the fields of anticounterfeiting, optogenetics, biosensing, therapeutics, etc. [25-28]. It is noteworthy that although MOF-based heterogeneous nanomaterials have made great progress, the reported approaches still encounter some limitations and challenges for inlaying anistropic nanorods with MOFs in a simply and efficient manner [29,30]. The typical method usually requires pretreatment or modification of nanorods to avoid the randomly agglomerated or nonuniform morphology [1,3l]. Moreover, these processes may also cause fluorescence-quenching and charge/energy transfer inhibition, which are unfavorable for their further functionalization and application [32, 33]. Therefore, there is an urgent need to develop new fabrication strategy to circumvent these restrictions for further advancing the applications of these MOF-nanorod composites.
Herein, we report a new approach to facilely prepare heterogeneous nanomaterials of MOF and upconversion nanorods (UCNRs) via in situ hierarchical and dynamic assembly without any pretreatment and modification of UCNRs (Scheme 1). The fabricated UiO-6 $6{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ display interesting core-satelite structures with UCNRs evenly anchored on the outer surface of MOFs, and successfully inherit the outstanding performance of both components. Benefiting from the luminescence and porous properties, these UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ composites were further applied for near-infrared (NIR)-triggered photodynamic therapy (PDT) via energy transfer from UCNRs to the photosensitizer (TCPP), and pH-triggered anticancer drug (5-fluorouraeil, Fu) delivery with negligible premature release. Cellular experiments demonstrated enhanced multimodal synergistic anticancer effect of these core-satellite structured MOF $@$ UCNRs composites, which hold great promise for cancer theranostics (Scheme 1).
