## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Synthesis and formation mechanism of UiO-66- NH@UCNRs
Then UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was chosen as the matrix material to synthesize the heterogeneous MOFs with nanorods. OA-capped UCNRs were directly mixed with $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ precursors and reacted at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ 、 The PXRD patterns (Fig.S5 in the ESM) confirm that the heterogeneous nanomaterials remain the crystal structures of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ and UCNRs. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Fig.1(c)) and TEM (Fig.1(d) and Fig. S6 in the
ESM) images reveal that the resulted heterogeneous nanomaterials possess octahedral geometry and uniformly distributed UCNRs on the surface. As shown in Fig.S7 in the ESM, the addition of OA-capped UCNRs did not affect the morphology of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ during the preparation of heterogeneous nanomaterials. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS） (Fig. S8 in the ESM) and elemental mapping images (Figs. l(e)-1(i)) display the distribution of elements in a single heterogeneous nanoparticle. The presence of primary elements of UCNRs and ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ i.e., F, O, Yb, Y, and $\mathrm{Zr}_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ further proved the efficient integration of UCNRs with UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . In contrast, only few UCNRs were observed in the center of the heterogeneous nanoparticle (Figs. 1(e), $1(\mathrm{g)}$ ，and $1(\mathrm{h})$ )，which indicates the inhomogeneous distribution of UCNRs in the nanocomposites. In order to acquire the exact distribution of UCNRs, the frozen section TEM technique was employed. The results showed that UCNRs only paved on the surface of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ and no UCNRs existed inside the UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ crystals (Fig.S9 in the ESM). As confirmed by dynamic light scattering (DLS), the mean diameter of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ is about $250~\mathrm{nm}$ 、and it increases to $383~\mathrm{nm}$ for the surface decorated UiO-6 $6{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Furthermore, the stability of UiO-66 $\mathsf{j}{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}@\mathsf{U C N R s}$ in PBS at various $\mathrm{pH}$ values was examined. Both SEM results (Fig. Sll in the ESM) and PXRD results (Fig.Sl2 in the ESM) indicated that the crystallinity and structural integrity of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ could be preserved in PBS solution for two days $\mathrm{(pH=7.4-5.4)}$
To explore the formation mechanism of the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ ，we first extracted the reaction mixture at different time intervals,which was characterized by SEM and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. Within $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ UCNRs showed a trend of gradual assembly in a plane with UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ precursors as the reaction progressed (Figs. 2(a)-2(c)). Meanwhile, the OA on the surface of UCNRs fell off stepwise, which was evidenced by FTIR spectroscopy (Fig. $2(\mathrm{g),}$ with the disappearance of peaks of $\mathrm{-CH}_{2}$ (2,926 and $2{,}853\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ）and $-C=0$ (1,560 and
$1{,}466~\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ . At a reaction time of $90~\mathrm{min}$ ， nucleation of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ occurred, and irregular and heterogeneous assemblies of UCNRs with UiO-66 $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystal nucleus appeared (Fig.2(d)). As time progressed, irregular and heterogeneous assemblies evolved into heterogeneous nanoparticles with polyhedron shape at $120~\mathrm{min}$ and grew up gradually (Figs. 2(e) and 2(f), indicating the growth of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . The appearance of new peaks from 1,200 to $1,800{\ c m}^{-1}$ in FTIR spectroscopy also indicates that UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was formed (Fig. $2(\mathrm{g)}^{\cdot}$ .Finally,UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ grew into octahedral nanoparticles with uniform size and UCNRs were paved on the surface (Fig. 1(c) and Fig. S9 in the ESM).
Based on the above results, we infer that the formation of UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ should follow three main stages via hierarchical and dynamic assembly as shown in Fig.2(h). Firstly, UCNRs assembled with $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ precursors via  supramolecular interactions (i.e., van der Waals interactions, electrostatic interactions, and hydrogen bonds) [35-38]， whichwas accompanied by the shedding of OA ligand, gradually inducing UCNRs to become positively charged [8]. Secondly, nucleation of UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ crystal and dynamic assembly with UCNRs occurred as the reaction proceeds. Under the driving force of heat, the nucleation of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ took place via coordination between 2-aminoterephthalic acid and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4},}$ along with the increase of the negativity [8]. Therefore, the plane supramolecular assembly dissociated, and positively charged UCNRs reassembled with negatively charged UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ crystal nucleus under the driving force of the enhanced electrostatic interactions, generating irregular and heterogeneous assemblies. Finally, UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ crystals grew and further assembled with UCNRs to yield UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ . Driven by chelation and heat, the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystal nucleus grew outward, and gradually grew into regular energetically favored octahedron shape as a result of Ostwald ripening [39]， generating the most negatively charged surface. Owing to the continually enhanced electrostatic interactions, irregular and heterogeneous assemblies dissociated and UCNRs redeposited onto the surface of energetically favored octahedral $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystals, leading to the formation of uniform UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ heterogeneous nanomaterials.
## 3.2 Optical properties and NIR light-triggered PDT
The successful inlay of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with UCNRs provides opportunities to construct novel nanocomposites with unique structures and multifunctionalities. As seen in Fig.3(a) and Fig. Sl3 in the ESM,the heterogeneous nanomaterials successfully inherited the typical luminescence behavior of UCNRs and UiO$66\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ allowing them suitable for further luminescence-based applications. PDT, as a new therapy strategy based on ROS (particularly singlet oxygen $^1\mathrm{O}_{2})$ , has attracted increasing attention in past decades due to its minimal invasion, high spatiotemporal precision, negligible drug resistance, and high selectivity [40-42]. However, the efficacy of PDT in the clinic was limited by the defect of hydrophobic photosensitizers, such as low tissue penetration depth of excitation light in ultraviolet to visible range, complicated  transport,  and self-quenchingcaused  by agglomeration in water, etc. Noteworthily, the outstanding upconversion luminescence performance and nanostructures of UiO-66 $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ allow us to design NIR light-triggered PDT via fluorescence resonance energy transfer (FRET) from
UCNRs to the photosensitizers, offering the potential to solve these limitations. In view of the short half-life and effective radius of ROS [43, 44], we grafted the porphyrinic photosensitizer TCPP onto the surface of $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ to guarantee the minimum distance between the photosensitizer and cells. Covalent condensation reaction between the carboxyl group of TCPP and amino group of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was carried out to avoid the loss of the photosensitizer from leakage and diffusion [45,46]. The khaki-colored UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ turned into deep brown UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP after TCPP decoration (Fig.Sl4 in the ESM). Based on the molecular size [47],large sized TCPP molecules (ca. $2\mathrm{nm}\cdot$ ）cannot be captured into the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ pores (diameter ca. $1.36~\mathrm{nm}$ ）[43].Thus, TCPP species are expected to locate on the surface of UiO- $66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ .The successful TCPP decoration was confirmed by fluorescence emission spectra. As shown in Fig.3(b), UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ UCNRsTCPP nanoparticles show strong emission at both 650 and $715\ \mathrm{nm}$ upon excitation at $420\ \mathrm{nm}.$ ：which confirms that TCPP molecules are conjugated to  the $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ successfully and the photosensitization property of TCPP is well preserved after surface decoration.
UV-vis absorption spectra were also used to verify the functionalization of UiO-66 $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ . The typical porphyrin sharp Soret band at $419\ \mathrm{nm}$ and four weak Q bands (520, 558, 599，and $652\ \mathrm{nm}$ ）also confirmed that UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ UCNRsTCPP was formed (Fig. S15 in the ESM). The PXRD patterns and morphology of UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP were the same as those of primitive $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs},$ implying that the structure of UiO-66-NH@UCNRs remains unchanged after the introduction of porphyrin species (Figs. S5 and Sl6 in the ESM). In addition, DLS (Fig.Si0 in the ESM) showed that the average diameter of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@\dot{}$ UCNRs-TCPP was slightly increased to $387\mathrm{nm}$
The UV-vis absorption spectrum of the TCPP overlaps with upconversion luminescence spectrum of UiO-6 $6{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ over a broad wavelength range (Fig. 3(c)), and the close proximity between TCPPwith $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ in UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ -TCPP enables the occurrence of FRET from UiO$66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ to TCPP. As shown in Fig.3(a), compared to UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs},$ the strong inhibition of the $\mathrm{Tm}^{3+}$ blue emission bands  after forming $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@U C N R s-T C P F}$ evidenced an efficient FRET between the pair. The increase in the ratio of upconversion luminescence intensity ( $\mathit{T}_{800}/I_{450}=3.3\$ and $I_{800}/I_{475}\quad=\quad3.0)$ of the UiO-6 $\mathsf{5-N H}_{2}@\mathsf{U C N R}$ s-TCPPin comparison with that of UiO- $66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathrm{UCNRs}}$ $\langle I_{800}/I_{450}=0.5$ and $I_{800}/I_{475}\ =\ 1.0\$ ） could be attributed to the remarkable overlap between the absorption of TCPP and the emission of UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ in the region of 400-500 nm (Fig. 3(c)).
Due to the energy transferred from UiO-66 $-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ to TCPP, it is reasoned that ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ could be produced from the UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs-TC}$ PP nanocomposites with $980~\mathrm{nm}$ NIR light irradiation (Fig. 3(e)), well within the tissue transparency window. Accordingly, SOsG was used to assess the production of ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ from the UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser excitation. The reagent SOSG reacts with ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to generate green fluorescence that can be characterized with fluorescence spectrophotometer. As shown in Fig.3(d), upon $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation,a significant increase of the fluorescence of SOSG at $525~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ was observed for UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP as irradiation time increased, indicating a steady singlet oxygen generation. As a control, UiO-66 $-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ and TCPP could not generate ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with unobvious fluorescence change under the $980\mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation.
The cellular uptakeof UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites by  HeLa cells  was further  investigated by fluorescence imaging. Fluorescence emission from the membrane tracker and $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was collected in two color channels, which are depicted in red and green (Fig.4(a)), respectively. The bright green luminescence from UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ segment in UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ UCNRs-TCPP ingested by HeLa cells could easily be observed in the cytoplasm (Fig.4(a)), suggesting that UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\stackrel{\cdot}{$ UCNRs-TCPP nanocomposites could be  effectively ingested by HeLa cells. Subsequently, DCFH-DA was used as an indicator to monitor intracellular ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation for different treatment groups. The cells treated with UiO $66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathrm{UCNRs}}$ TCPP under $980~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ laser irradiation showed bright green fluorescence, indicating the ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation (Fig. 4(b)). In contrast, no obvious green fluorescence was seen for cells treated with nonirradiated UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\$ UCNRs-TCPP.
## 3.3 pH-triggered drug delivery and synergistic anticancer effect
Smart drug delivery and multimodal therapy have attracted increasing attentions due to their potential to realize enhanced anticancer effect [48-50]. The high surface areas and porosity of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ inspire us to further exploit the smart drug delivery performance of the fabricated UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ UCNRs-TCPP.In order to deliver anticancer drugs, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption analysis was used to measure the surface area of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ UiO-66 $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs},$ and UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP. As indicated in Figs.Sl7and Sl8inthe ESM,the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface areas of ${\mathrm{UiO}}{-}66{-}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ UiO-66 $\mathrm{\partial_{\cdot}N H_{2}}@\mathrm{UCNRs},$ and UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP were calculated to be 712, 515, and 491 $\mathbf{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ， respectively. Compared to the pristine $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ the obvious decline in the surface area for UiO-6 $\mathsf{\backslash6-N H_{2}@U C N R s}$ is likely caused by  the incorporation of nonporous UCNRs. Meanwhile, only a slight decrease of the surface area for UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP was found compared to $\mathrm{UiO-66-NH_{2}@U C N R s},$ which further confirmed that TCPP molecules are located on the surface of UiO$66{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}@{\mathrm{UCNRs}},$ rather than inside the pores. Moreover, the typical type-I adsorption isothermal features and pore size distributions were basically the same as $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fig. S19 in the ESM),which is suitable for drug encapsulation [45]. The porous nanocomposites exhibited positive charge in water, thus negatively-charged chemotherapy drugs (i.e., Fu) could be loaded into the pores through electrostatic interactions (Fig.S2O in the ESM) to achieve the final nanocomposites Fu@UiO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ -TCPP. The loading efficiency of Fu was calculated to be $6.5\mathrm{wt}.\%$ through the UV-vis absorption spectroscopy (Figs. S21 and S22(d) in the ESM). The electrostatic interaction-induced drug encapsulation makes pH-triggered release possible, which could minimize undesired release and command the effective release of the antitumor drug in tumor cells with lower $\mathrm{pH}$ level [5l]. The Fu release profiles at different $\mathrm{\pH}$ values were shown in Fig.5(a) and Fig.S22 in the ESM. Fu molecules could be firmly capped within the nanopores at $\mathrm{pH}=7.4,$ exhibiting negligible premature release (less than $5\%$ . When lowering the $\mathrm{pH}$ to 6.4, slight release of Fu could be observed, indicating $\mathrm{pH}$ can trigger the release process. At a more acidic $\mathrm{pH}$ of 5.4, the release rate of Fu became much faster, and over $70\%$ of Fu was released within $60\mathrm{min}$
Furthermore, the cytotoxicity of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\$ UCNRs-TCPP before and after the drug encapsulation was investigated by using CCK-8 assay against HeLa cells at various concentrations with or without $980\ \mathrm{nm}$ laser irradiation for $10~\mathrm{min}$ .As shown in Fig.5(b) and Fig.S23 in the ESM, the viability of cells treated with UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@\dot{}$ UCNRs-TCPP remained above $97\%$ in the absence of light irradiation, indicating that UiO-6 $_{\mathrm{6-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}}.$ TCPP possessed excellent biocompatibility and negligible dark cytotoxicity. $\mathrm{Fu}@\mathrm{UiO}\ –66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ -TCPP showed high dark cytotoxicity, and the cell viability decreased up to $50\%$ ，indicating the pH-triggered release of antitumor drug Fu in HeLa cells. Particularly, the toxicity of UiO-66-NH@UCNRs-TCPP was observed upon NIR irradiations, resulting more than $60\%$ cell death due to the ${}^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generation. Notably, incubation of HeLa cells with $\mathrm{Fu}@\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}.$ TCPP following NIR irradiation led to even higher cytotoxicity (up to $90\%$ cell death) than that of the cells treated with either $\mathrm{Fu@UiO–66–NH_{2}@U C N R s}$ TCPP without irradiation or UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ UCNRs-TCPPwith irradiation, indicating an enhanced anticancer efficiency could be achieved through the combination of chemotherapy and NIRinduced PDT.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we have developed a new strategy to fabricate MOFbased heterogeneous nanomaterials with  surface-anchored UCNRs via hierarchical and dynamic assembly. The formation mechanism of these UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\dot{}$ UCNRs heterogeneous nanocomposites follows three main stages: supramolecular assembly of UiO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ precursors with UCNRs, nucleation and growth of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ crystal, and dynamic assembly with UCNRs accompanied by Ostwald ripening. The heterogeneous nanocomposites successfully inherited the merits of both components, including distinct luminescence, high surface area, and porous structure. Consequently, NIR-triggered PDT platform has been established based on energy transfer from UCNRs to
TCPP for potential anticancer applications. Upon further encapsulating anti-cancer drugs Fu into the pores via electrostatic interactions, $\mathrm{Fu}@\mathrm{UiO}\ –66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}@\mathrm{UCNRs}$ exhibit excellent pHtriggered release and enhanced multimodal synergistic anticancer effect by combining NIR-induced PDT with chemotherapy. The present strategy of fabricating heterogeneous nanomaterials via hierarchical and dynamic assembly is simple and controllable, which can be also extended to the development of other functional composite materials.
